ncing off the flag will eventually smash into interstellar dust particles. When they hit the dust, they scatter. Once a photon scatters, the visual information it was carrying is permanently destroyed. No telescope, no matter how impossibly huge, can piece together an image if the light carrying that image no longer exists. **3. The Quantum Noise Limit** Let's say the flag is on a planet orbiting a distant star. To see the flag, you have to look through the glare of that star. The star is billions of times brighter than the tiny amount of light reflecting off the flag. You might think an advanced civilization could just use a digital filter to block the star's light. But because of quantum mechanics, light fluctuates randomly. The natural, unpredictable fluctuation of the star's light (called "photon noise") is actually *larger* than the entire signal coming from the flag. The flag's light is mathematically erased by the quantum static of the star. **4. The Time Machine Problem** Finally, there is the speed of light. Let's say you want to look at a flag in the Andromeda Galaxy, 2.5 million light-years away. Because light takes time to travel, you aren't looking at the galaxy as it is right now; you are looking at it as it was 2.5 million years ago. Even if you had a telescope the size of a galaxy, and even if you could somehow solve the photon, dust, and quantum problems... you still couldn't see the flag, because 2.5 million years ago, the flag hadn't been made yet. **The Ultimate Verdict** Your logic is flawless when it comes to the mechanics of telescopes: bigger equals better, and engineering has no theoretical limit. But seeing requires two things: a telescope, and light. While we could theoretically build a telescope of infinite size, **light itself degrades, scatters, and runs out.** That is the ultimate physical wall that stops us from seeing anything, at any size, at any distance!
